{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     services/analytics — the ONE include point for every third-party script.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     default.hbs includes this once, at the end of <body>, and nothing else
     in the theme may load a third-party script. One include point is what
     makes "what does this site send, and to whom" answerable by reading one
     file — the failure it prevents is the tag that three templates each
     added once and nobody can find when a reader asks why the site talks to
     a host they have never heard of.

     END OF BODY, DELIBERATELY, FOR ALL OF THEM. The head-placement argument
     for analytics is "count the visitors who bounce before the page
     finishes"; the cost is a script fetched and parsed before the reading
     surface paints. gtag.js is ~417 KB of JavaScript and PostHog ~267 KB —
     each larger than the theme's entire stylesheet — and a learning site
     that trades LCP for catching sub-second bounces has the trade backwards.
     No service here needs the head: GA4 and PostHog only timestamp a little
     later, and OneSignal's SDK is defer by design. A service that genuinely
     required head placement would be included from default.hbs's <head>
     directly and would have to argue for it in its own file; none does.

     ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. consent must run before ga4 and posthog: they
     probe window.NSConsent synchronously, and if the gate is absent they
     conclude the site has no consent requirement and load immediately.
     These are all inline (or deferred) scripts in source order, so the
     ordering is guaranteed by this file and by nothing else.

     The consent banner only earns its pixels when something is actually
     gated, so it renders only when consent is required AND at least one
     gated service is configured. Handlebars has no `or`, hence the nested
     ifs — ugly, but the alternative is a cookie banner on a site that sets
     no cookies, which is worse than ugly. OneSignal sits outside the gate:
     its browser permission prompt IS its consent moment (see its file).

     Every service below no-ops when its setting is empty, so including this
     partial on a site with no settings configured renders exactly nothing.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if @custom.require_analytics_consent}}
    {{#if @custom.ga4_id}}
        {{> "services/consent"}}
    {{else}}
        {{#if @custom.posthog_key}}
            {{> "services/consent"}}
        {{/if}}
    {{/if}}
{{/if}}
{{> "services/ga4"}}
{{> "services/posthog"}}
{{> "services/onesignal"}}
